.TH "REGEXP.MULTILINE" "3JS" "August 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
RegExp.multiline \- RegExp.prototype.multiline
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]multiline\f[B]\f[R] accessor property of
\f[CR]RegExp\f[R] instances returns whether or not the \f[CR]m\f[R] flag
is used with this regular expression.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] regex1 = /\(hafootball/;
\f[B]const\f[R] regex2 = /\(hafootball/m;

console.log(regex1.multiline);
\f[I]// Expected output: false\f[R]

console.log(regex2.multiline);
\f[I]// Expected output: true\f[R]

console.log(regex1.test(\(dqrugby\(rsnfootball\(dq));
\f[I]// Expected output: false\f[R]

console.log(regex2.test(\(dqrugby\(rsnfootball\(dq));
\f[I]// Expected output: true\f[R]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]RegExp.prototype.multiline\f[R] has the value \f[CR]true\f[R] if
the \f[CR]m\f[R] flag was used; otherwise, \f[CR]false\f[R].
The \f[CR]m\f[R] flag indicates that a multiline input string should be
treated as multiple lines.
For example, if \f[CR]m\f[R] is used, \f[CR]\(ha\f[R] and \f[CR]$\f[R]
change from matching at only the start or end of the entire string to
the start or end of any line within the string.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] To match the start and end of the entire string in
\f[CR]m\f[R] mode, use the buffer boundary assertions \f[CR]\(rsA\f[R],
\f[CR]\(rsz\f[R], and \f[CR]\(rsZ\f[R].
.RE
.PP
The set accessor of \f[CR]multiline\f[R] is \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
You cannot change this property directly.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using multiline
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] regex = /\(hafoo/m;

console.log(regex.multiline); \f[I]// true\f[R]
.EE
